\section{TS objects}{
The basic object in 'RserveTS' is a \code{ts_object}. It carries an input type,
a return type, an optional default, and a \code{check()} helper used on the R
side during development.

Input types describe the 'zod' schema of objects that 'TypeScript' can pass
to 'Rserve' functions. Return types describe the 'zod' schema of objects
that 'Rserve' functions return; most utilise the \code{Robj} helpers in the
'rserve-ts' library (with \code{r_type} and \code{r_attributes}).
}

\section{Scalar versus array ("vector") types}{
In R, almost all types are vectors. In the 'rserve-js' library, primitive
arrays of length one are converted into scalars, which leads to type
checking issues when a return value has unknown length (e.g. \code{which(x > 5)}).

For vectors that support this distinction, pass \code{n}:
\itemize{
\item \code{n = 1} -- scalar form
\item \code{n != 1} (including \code{0}) -- array form
\item default (\code{n = -1}) -- union of scalar and array forms
}

This applies to logicals, integers, numerics, and characters.
}

\section{Atomic types}{
\itemize{
\item \code{ts_logical()}: logical / boolean. Array form: \code{Int8Array} /
\code{Uint8Array}.
\item \code{ts_integer()}: integer. Array form: \code{Int32Array}. 'JavaScript' has no
native integer type, so scalars are numbers (same as \code{ts_numeric()}).
\item \code{ts_numeric()}: numeric / double. Array form: \code{Float64Array}.
\item \code{ts_character()}: character / string. Array form: \code{string[]}.
\item \code{ts_factor()}: factor. Always a string array on the 'JavaScript' side
(even for a single value); optional \code{levels} constrain the labels.
}
}

\section{Structured types}{
\itemize{
\item \code{ts_list()}: a list (named object or array in 'JavaScript'). Forms:
\enumerate{
\item Unknown list -- \code{ts_list()}
\item Known named list -- \code{ts_list(x = ts_integer(), y = ts_character())}
(object in 'JavaScript')
\item Known unnamed list -- \code{ts_list(ts_integer(), ts_character())}
(array in 'JavaScript')
\item Named list of one value type with unknown keys -- use
\code{ts_record(value_type)} (\verb{Record<string, type>} in 'TypeScript')
\item Unnamed list of one value type with unknown length --
\code{ts_list(ts_integer())}-style homogeneous arrays (\verb{Array<type>})
}
\item \code{ts_record()}: named list whose values share one type
(\verb{Record<string, value_type>}).
\item \code{ts_dataframe()}: data frame; named columns of equal length.
}
